          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Lorck\",\"addressee\":\"Constance\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows Lorck's present love for Constance. He immediately insists that it is serious and deeper than he himself had understood, making presentation of an emotional state its clear dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"In this occurrence, the avowal introduces Lorck's offer of his whole self, life, and soul and his request that Constance accept them. Thus the expression does more than accompany a separate promise: within the same continuous appeal it affirms that he is offering himself as bound to an intimate relationship with her. The terms remain figurative and unspecified, so undertaking force is substantial rather than fully determinate.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Although Lorck is emotionally agitated, the focal words are a controlled, intelligible avowal used to persuade Constance and support his offer. The evidence does not portray them as a reflex-like cry or expressive discharge beyond their assertive and undertaking functions.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Lorck korsede Armene paa Brystet, og\\nbetragtede hende stift.\\n\\n,Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.87,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's force is adequately captured by avowal and contextual undertaking. Its persuasive role in seeking acceptance does not establish a distinct core mode beyond T and P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\\nJeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lorck speaks privately to Constance after declaring a consuming passion, kneeling before her, and urging her to yield to him. She rejects that advance and asks him to stop; after she appears frightened and he demands to know whether she is sending him away, he delivers the focal avowal as a renewed, serious appeal and offers himself to her.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Lorck offers Constance himself, his life, and his soul, asking her to accept this proposed intimate bond. Constance immediately refuses—“Nej, uf nej”—and withdraws, so the offer receives explicit negative uptake. The language indicates a sweeping relational undertaking, but its practical terms are not specified, and his subsequent aggression complicates its character without removing its original undertaking force.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text identifies the work as Amalie Skram's Constance Ring, which I recognise.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports that Lorck experiences infatuation or passion, but it does not conclusively establish the sincerity, durability, or ethical quality of his professed love. Later supplied material may bear on his conduct, but it does not erase the avowal and rejected undertaking made in this scene.\"}"
